[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: An evidence node whose SOURCE is a URL goes dirty when the page behind it changes

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SOURCE-URL-DRIFT-CHECK
TITLE: An evidence node whose SOURCE is a URL goes dirty when the page behind it changes
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
An EVIDENCE node may name an external source -- a vendor's documentation
page, a paper, a changelog -- in SOURCE. Nothing today notices when that
page changes. The mechanism: a check fetches each URL-shaped SOURCE, hashes
the fetched body (normalised enough to ignore trivial churn), records the
hash beside the entry, and reports the node as drifted when the hash moves,
the way the fingerprint check reports a child whose parent's contract moved.
Whether the hash lives in the node, in a sidecar, or in the export cache is
the design question; so is what counts as trivial churn and whether the
fetch runs in nix flake check at all (it cannot, offline) or only in a
devenv task.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator's suggestion of 2026-08-30: a URL reference that can go dirty is not a bad idea as a special case, because upstream documentation changes underneath a citation.
<<<
